<h2 id="ac">Acceptance criteria</h2>

<h4>Select Package screen</h4>
<ul class="ac">
  <li>The price label reads <strong>From</strong>, not "Total price"</li>
  <li>The figure equals the exact total of the cheapest arrangement that seats the searched party</li>
  <li>Each card states how many arrangements fit the party</li>
  <li>Inclusions — breakfast, taxes, transfers — appear on the card</li>
  <li>Search takes rooms, adults and children with ages, matching screen 3's hotel search</li>
  <li>Packages that cannot seat the party are dimmed with a reason, or excluded</li>
</ul>

<h4>Room combinations</h4>
<ul class="ac">
  <li>One table per room type, with screen 3's four columns: Board &amp; Policy · Number Of Guests · Price Per Night · Select Room</li>
  <li>Bed-type chips and "See room details" carry over from screen 3</li>
  <li>A quantity stepper on every rate row</li>
  <li>A running summary shows each selected room, its occupants, and <code>rate × nights = subtotal</code></li>
  <li>A guests-placed counter is visible at all times and gates Proceed To Payment</li>
  <li>Selecting capacity beyond the party size is blocked or flagged</li>
  <li>Child placement is stated on the row and priced from the age band</li>
  <li>Sold-out or restricted rates are greyed with the reason retained</li>
  <li>The cheapest arrangement is preselected, matches the results card, and has a one-tap return</li>
</ul>

<h4>Add-ons and price</h4>
<ul class="ac">
  <li>Each add-on shows its arithmetic, e.g. <code>150 × 5 guests × 6 nights</code></li>
  <li>Per-unit add-ons have their own quantity stepper</li>
  <li>Selected add-ons appear as separate lines above Discount</li>
  <li>Toggling updates the total immediately with no apply step</li>
  <li>TAX recalculates from the new base every time</li>
  <li>The panel keeps today's four lines: Package Price · Discount · TAX · Total Include vat</li>
  <li>The total carried to payment equals the last total displayed</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="questions">Open questions</h2>
<p class="lead">The first two block design. The rest block build.</p>

<ul class="q">
  <li><b>Do add-ons enter the payment, or stay as they are?</b> Today they're explicitly outside it, arranged by WhatsApp. Four of the six are priced "starting from", which cannot be summed into a firm total. If they stay outside, wireframe 4 becomes a read-only list and the price panel barely changes — a much smaller piece of work.</li>
  <li><b>Option A or Option B for room selection?</b> You described A — self-assembled from a room table, matching screen 3. B generates arrangements. A is closer to the existing site; B asks less of the traveller. Either way the cheapest calculation is needed for the "From" price.</li>
  <li><b>A package that can't seat the party — dimmed or hidden?</b> Hidden is cleaner; dimmed with a reason helps a traveller understand a thin result set.</li>
  <li><b>Are add-ons taxed at the same 15%?</b> The railway is a third-party ticket and may be treated differently. Finance question, and it changes the TAX line.</li>
  <li><b>Is the requested room count binding?</b> The search asks for 2 rooms. If the cheapest arrangement needs 3, do we show it?</li>
  <li><b>How are children handled in the search?</b> Screen 2 currently shows only "Passengers: 1" with no child concept. Screen 3's hotel search has Adults and Children. The package search must adopt that before any of this works.</li>
  <li><b>Is there a hold while the traveller assembles?</b> Building an arrangement across three room types takes minutes. Without a hold, a row can sell out mid-decision.</li>
  <li><b>Extra nights after 18 October — fixed price or a range?</b> The live copy says "rates start from", implying variable. That changes the control from a stepper to a date picker.</li>
</ul>

<div class="note ok">
  <b>For the UX designer</b>
  <p>The wireframes are greyscale on purpose — structure only, your visual language. Purple marks what's new against the live screens. What I'd ask you to preserve: the <strong>four-column room table from screen 3</strong> so it feels like the rest of the site, the <strong>running arrangement summary</strong> with <code>rate × nights</code> visible, the <strong>guests-placed counter as the gate</strong> on Proceed, and the <strong>existing four price lines</strong> — Package Price, Discount, TAX, Total Include vat. Everything else is open.</p>
</div>

